now the link between constituency and mp  is obviously in sharp focus this weekend  not every former prime minister enjoyed  staying on the back benches after their  time in number 10 but gordon brown did  and he was there for a further five  years as mp for coccody and cowden beef  he joins me now from  fife  uh good morning uh gordon brown  do you  have a memory of  david amos  yes my first thoughts of course are with  his widow julia and their five children  this is a terrible tragedy i remember  david entered the same same time as me  to the house of commons  i think he was the person i remember  most because almost every question and  every speech he made he always mentioned  the name of his constituency and that  shows just how much he was in touch with  his local community hard hard-working  determined caring compassionate we've  heard all this that emphasizes just what  a good man of the people he actually was  and this is beyond partisanship  you've got to pay respects to someone  who really  died doing something that he enjoyed  most which was listening to  consulting  uh helping  serving his constituents and i will  remember him as a as a great  constituency mp as i know everybody else  will who is in the house of commons  today  also  what of course has emerged in the  discussion of the aftermath of this  dreadful event  is the  relationship between  the member of parliament and the  constituency  is there a danger that we now find  ourselves  willingly  having the culture of our democracy in  some way eroded because of because  public figures have to retreat behind a  wall of security of some kind  yet trevor trevor this is an attack on  our democracy  so the answer cannot be less democracy  this looks as if it is a terrorist  incident  it is probably related to al-shabaab or  to al-qaeda and we'll find out more in  the next few days  but the answer when you come across a  terrorist incident  is we don't blink we don't shirk we  don't flinch we don't show weakness  we stand up for what we believe  and of course we will increase the  security that is available to members of  parliament and councillors when they're  doing the surgeries and that is  something that must happen immediately  but it's true also to say that we must  not allow our democracy to be diminished  because the terrorist attack has  occurred i think the history of dealing  with terrorism is that if you ever show  weakness and you ever give the  impression that coexistence is  impossible and that you're going to back  away and give up on your values  then you show something that gives  terrorist hope and we must not do that  you know hearts are broken but our  resolve must be unbreakable  a life has been tragically destroyed but  our values our democratic values they  are indestructible and we must show that  now  if you were in government now would you  consider for example you as a former  prime minister i think  are entitled to close protection i  bodyguards and so on  would you  favor  making that possibility available to  members of parliament now  yes because if there is a risk to  anybody who is a member of the general  public or a member of parliament  and the risk is apparent to the police  authorities  they will do everything in their power  to protect that person and i think what  the speaker is doing and harriet  harmon's called for a speaker's  conference on this  is absolutely right we must say to  members of parliament of course be  careful where you hold your constituency  surgeries in a place that is protected  but at the same time if you need police  protection uh that is going to be  available  and it need not be obtrusive it need not  be so obvious but i think the protection  has got to be made available now to  members of parliament who feel that  there is a risk to what they are doing  and i think that will be introduced in  the next few days  one of the things that i imagine that  david david amerson we both knew him  well would probably be saying  though  he's a man of great compassion kindness  and  if this had happened to somebody else he  would be the first to express his sorrow  but he i guess he would also be saying  that  events should not come in  the way of uh public servants  continuing to do  what the important things they need to  do looking after their constituents and  i want to talk to you about one of the  human issues that you've raised which is  of course  the impact of covert particularly in  developing countries  when we last spoke which is back in june  uh you  said that there were millions of doses  of uh vaccines which you thought should  be shipped to developing countries uh  were sitting in storehouses in europe um  where are we on that now  but you're right to ask this question  because  we've got a detailed report appearing  tomorrow  that shows there are 240 million  vaccines  that are lying in europe and america  that are unused not likely to be used  for months many of them may go to waste  and that would be criminal which could  immediately be airlifted out to those  countries where the level of vaccination  is so low  that not even the nurses and doctors are  protected and certainly not the elderly  vulnerable  and you know boris johnson promised at  the g7  that he was going to vaccinate the whole  world he made this bold announcement  that by next year everybody would be  vaccinated who was an adult in the  developing world as well as the  developed world  but since then so little has happened  that we now face the possibility that  every  target is being missed 10 percent by  september missed 40 percent by december  likely to be missed 70 by next year  likely to be missed and so we've got to  take action immediately  to use these unused vaccines  to save lives you know 100 000 lives  have been saved in britain because of  100 million vaccines how many more lives  can be saved in the rest of the world if  we get these vaccines to people who need  them  you um  if i may  say so you used the word criminal there  in an answer which you also refer to the  prime minister  why do you think this hasn't happened  is it just incompetence or is there  something deeper going on here  i i i was talking about neglect  uh and i do think that this is a lack of  coordination  if president biden boris johnson or sula  van laden at the european union  justin trudeau if they came together  they would find that they had these  unused vaccines  that they're not going to use them even  after all the boosters they are excess  vaccines that are not going to be used  and they should get them out as quickly  as possible  and month by month this number of ex  extra vaccines will grow  another 200 million next month 200  million by the end of the year a billion  in total by january now we've got to get  them to the people who need them  otherwise  people will say  that the neglect is unacceptable if a  hundred million or so vaccines as we  predict go to waste because they pass  the use by date this is the biggest  public policy failure at an  international level for years  outside their war in peace time and  really to save hundreds of thousands of  lives by using the vaccine the miracle  of which is available to us  is something that needs the coordination  of the leaders only they can release the  vaccines not the imf or the world health  organization the leaders have got to  make that decision they ordered the  vaccines they over-ordered and they can  now make them available  if i recall correctly when we discussed  it before actually on the  very day  i think that the president of the united  states was calling a meeting  of uh his fellow g7 leaders to do  exactly what you've just said  why didn't that  happen i mean why didn't we hear more  about that  well i think it was a tragedy that the  meeting  went round the table i don't think boris  johnson was there from the information  that i have although he was down to  speak as right at the beginning of the  event it went round the table people  made promises but they didn't add up to  being enough to get the vaccinations to  people at the time that they needed them  and so we have promises about next year  and we have some vaccines going this  year but we'll never meet the 40 target  unless we take action now and that's an  airlift it needs an airlift now  boris did you say that boris johnson  wasn't in the room when they were  talking about this  well downing street has refused to  confirm that he was there and yet he  made the promise that he was going to  get the world vaccinated and the big  summit that was to be held and was held  under the office of president biden if  as people are saying he did not attend  then i think he's better answer to the  house of commons about why a promise was  made at the g7 that he was going to  oversee and then when it came to the  meetings that had to deal with at a  vaccine summit including the secretary  general of the united nations president  of the european union uh the prime  minister of canada everybody else was  there prime minister draghi of italy  we have to ask what role did he play in  that summit  can i just uh briefly return to the uh  issue of this morning  and ask you about the tone of politics  right now  i spoke both to the home secretary and  to lisa nandy about this and both of  them  i think seem to  confirm what a lot of people are saying  that  without wanting to trace this particular  event to  uh you know the wider  political discourse that there is some  an edge an unpleasantness a coarseness  that's entered  uh political speech and debate  which uh  from their point of view we could well  do without what is your view  yeah i think you can disagree without  being disagreeable and i think  you can have discord without having  malice  and too often i think political  arguments descend  into personal  attacks and personal  conflicts  and i think when it comes to the social  media we've got to look again at the way  these algorithms are organized  because they push people towards the  most extreme positions  and and the media looks to me like a  shouting match without an umpire but the  most extreme positions are the ones that  come across  now this is i think partly because of  the way that um the different media  organizations organize their information  and i think something's got to be done  to look at  online harm that is resulting from this  so yes we've got to clean up here but  let us also remember that if this is a  terrorist incident  we are dealing with a group of  terrorists or group  around a terrorist  who believe that coexistence with the  west is impossible  and we have got to show by the strength  of our democracy and the strength by  which we reach out to other countries  and call for cooperation that this is  not the way that we see the world and  this is not the way the world has got to  be  terrorism has got to be fought directly  head-on  gordon brown thank you very much indeed  thank you
 

